<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating an Integral by Trig substitution</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use trigonometric substitution to evaluate</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle\int{@question_func@}dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<p><em>Note: \(\displaystyle \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{a}{b}\right)\) can be typed as: <code>atan(a/b)</code> and \(\displaystyle\sqrt{a-b}\) can be typed as: <code>sqrt(a-b)</code></em>.</p>
<p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>\( \text{Worked Solution}\)</p>
<p></p>
<p>
</p>
<p> \( \text{In order to carry out trig substitution integrals, let's take a look at the information in the table below.} \)<br></p>
<div>
    <table width="750" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="0" border="1">
        <colgroup>
            <col>
            <col>
            <col>
            <col>
        </colgroup>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\( \text{If you see }\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\( \text{Use the following substitution}\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\text{Where}\)</p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\text{So that,}\)</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{a^2-x^2}\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;"> \(x=a\sin(\theta)\)</p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(dx=a\cos(\theta)d\theta\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{a^2-x^2} = a\cos(\theta)\)<br></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{a^2+x^2}\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(x=a\tan(\theta)\)</p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(dx=a\sec^{2}(\theta)d\theta\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{a^2+x^2} = a\sec(\theta)\)<br></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{x^2-a^2}\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(x=a\sec(\theta)\)</p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(dx=a\sec(\theta)\tan(\theta)d\theta\)<br></p>
                </td>
                <td>
                    <p dir="ltr" style="text-align: left;">\(\sqrt{x^2-a^2} = a\tan(\theta)\)<br></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p></p>
<p>Looking at the numerator of the integrand {@sqrt(num_func)@}, it seems we'll need the following trig substitution </p>
<p>Let \( x= {@let_x@}\). Then&nbsp; \( dx={@diff_let_x@}d(\theta) \)</p>
<p>Now let us substitute for \(x\) in the numerator as shown. </p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
    {@sqrt(num_func)@} &amp; =\displaystyle \sqrt {{@coef1^2@} \left({@let_x@}\right)^{2}-{@coef2^2@}} \\
    &amp; = \displaystyle {@new_expr_subst_let_x@} \\
    &amp; =\displaystyle {@factor_new_expr_subst_let_x@} \\
    &amp; =\displaystyle  {@coef2@} \displaystyle \sqrt{ \tan^{2}(\theta)} &amp;&amp; \text{since, } \tan^{2}(\theta)=\sec^{2}(\theta)-1\\
    &amp; =\displaystyle  {@expr_interms_of_tan@} \\
    &amp; = \displaystyle {@expr_interms_of_tan_no_abs1@} &amp;&amp; \text{By assuming, } \tan(\theta) &gt; 0 
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>Now going back to the question and substituting everything, we'll have,</p>
<p></p>
<p> \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle\int {@question_func@} dx &amp;= \displaystyle \int \frac{{@expr_interms_of_tan_no_abs1@}}{@coef3*let_x@}\cdot {@diff_let_x@}d(\theta)\\
    &amp;= \displaystyle \int{@new_question_func_trig@}d(\theta) \\
    &amp;= \displaystyle {@int_new_question_func_trig@}
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>But \(\displaystyle x= {@let_x@}\) and \(\displaystyle {@expression_sin_theta@}\). We need to compute \(\displaystyle  \tan(\theta)\) and \( (\theta)\), then substitute them.</p>
<p>Recall that \(\displaystyle  \tan^{2}(\theta)+1=\sec^{2}(\theta)={@expression_sin_theta_rhs^2@}\)</p>
<p>So, </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle {@expression_tan_trig@} \)</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle {@solve_tan_expression_tan_trig@} \)</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle {@solve_tan_expression_tan_trig1@} \) and \( \displaystyle {@solve_theta_expression_tan_trig1@} \) (note that \( \displaystyle \text{atan(u)} =\tan^{-1}(u), u \in \mathbb{R}\) )</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p> \(
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle\int {@question_func@} dx &amp; = {@int_new_question_func_trig@}\\
    &amp; = \displaystyle {@final_answer_subst@}
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p> Where \(c\) is a constant of integration. </p>]]></text>
